WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.963
Welcome back to Building
Global Secure Access.

2
00:00:02.963 --> 00:00:07.249
This is Chapter 5: the compliant
network, and tenant restrictions.

3
00:00:07.249 --> 00:00:10.917
These are two settings only
Microsoft Entra can provide,

4
00:00:10.917 --> 00:00:13.725
and two of the easiest
ways to lock users out.

5
00:00:13.725 --> 00:00:17.250
The code's job is to say when
each is safe to enforce.

6
00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:21.896
We'll start from Learn's example policy
and its universal tenant restrictions,

7
00:00:21.896 --> 00:00:23.397
see what they leave to you,

8
00:00:23.397 --> 00:00:27.400
read eleven checks and the plan's
enforcement gate, and walk two labs.

9
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.914
The chapter is a draft awaiting review,

10
00:00:29.914 --> 00:00:33.800
and the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:38.475
Why a gate?

12
00:00:38.475 --> 00:00:43.419
The compliant network check passes only
for users who come through the agency's

13
00:00:43.419 --> 00:00:48.063
own Global Secure Access, so a replayed
token from anywhere else is refused.

14
00:00:48.063 --> 00:00:53.637
Tenant restrictions let agency devices
sign in only to tenants the policy allows.

15
00:00:53.637 --> 00:00:57.674
Both are strong, and both can lock people
out if they're enforced too early.

16
00:00:57.674 --> 00:01:00.702
So the goal: keep both as
reviewed configuration,

17
00:01:00.702 --> 00:01:04.410
show when the compliant network
policy is ready to enforce,

18
00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:08.950
and show that agency devices reach
only the tenants the register allows.

19
00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:11.694
It's for the developers,
the identity engineer,

20
00:01:11.694 --> 00:01:14.810
and the policy owner
who decides enforcement.

21
00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:19.860
Learn gives a lot here.

22
00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:25.195
Its example policy blocks everything
outside the All Compliant Network locations

23
00:01:25.195 --> 00:01:28.778
named location, excludes the
two Intune applications so

24
00:01:28.778 --> 00:01:32.360
devices can still enroll, and
excludes emergency access.

25
00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:35.150
The check isn't supported
for Private Access apps,

26
00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:39.097
and turning signaling off while
policies depend on it can block users.

27
00:01:39.097 --> 00:01:42.260
The named location is listed
with Policy Read All.

28
00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:46.446
Universal tenant restrictions
apply the TRv2 policy to traffic

29
00:01:46.446 --> 00:01:50.322
the Microsoft profile carries,
through one tagging setting.

30
00:01:50.322 --> 00:01:54.681
Learn's tests are disabling the client
and signing in again, and a four-case

31
00:01:54.681 --> 00:01:59.750
matrix for tenants, with a block query
for error five million two hundred eleven.

32
00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:04.725
What does it leave to you?

33
00:02:04.725 --> 00:02:07.569
Learn's example policy
is on from the start,

34
00:02:07.569 --> 00:02:12.850
but the guide begins every policy in
report-only, so the kit's file is report-only.

35
00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:15.184
Nothing says when report-only is done,

36
00:02:15.184 --> 00:02:19.776
so the kit's rule is ten business days
and no failure carried by the service,

37
00:02:19.776 --> 00:02:23.088
and the plan won't enforce
without an explicit switch.

38
00:02:23.088 --> 00:02:26.272
Exclusions are prose, so
two checks look for them

39
00:02:26.272 --> 00:02:29.775
and the plan refuses a file
without the emergency one.

40
00:02:29.775 --> 00:02:33.361
The tagging toggle enforces
nothing with an allowing default,

41
00:02:33.361 --> 00:02:35.637
so tagging waits for a blocking default.

42
00:02:35.637 --> 00:02:39.510
And every partner allowance needs
a register row with an owner.

43
00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:44.373
Eleven checks.

44
00:02:44.373 --> 00:02:47.781
For the compliant network:
the named location exists,

45
00:02:47.781 --> 00:02:52.971
which tells you signaling was turned on;
the policy blocks everything outside it;

46
00:02:52.971 --> 00:02:56.457
the emergency group and both
Intune apps are excluded;

47
00:02:56.457 --> 00:02:59.942
no Private Access app is in
scope; while it's report-only,

48
00:02:59.942 --> 00:03:02.886
the business days and
the defects are counted,

49
00:03:02.886 --> 00:03:07.611
as information until it's ready; and once
it's enforced, signaling must be on.

50
00:03:07.611 --> 00:03:11.928
For tenant restrictions: the default
blocks everyone and everything;

51
00:03:11.928 --> 00:03:16.694
each partner allowance is in the register
and current; tagging is on only with

52
00:03:16.694 --> 00:03:21.670
a blocking default; and each blocked
tenant is listed for the weekly review.

53
00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:26.445
Who decides?

54
00:03:26.445 --> 00:03:31.446
The policy owner decides enforcement, and
passes the enforce switch on the record,

55
00:03:31.446 --> 00:03:34.020
only after the report-only
result is clean.

56
00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:38.056
The identity engineer runs the
policies and owns the partner list,

57
00:03:38.056 --> 00:03:40.257
reviewed with the partner register.

58
00:03:40.257 --> 00:03:44.487
The security monitor watches blocks
that weren't carried by the service:

59
00:03:44.487 --> 00:03:47.095
those are either an attack
or a broken client.

60
00:03:47.095 --> 00:03:49.959
For permissions: Policy
Read All for policies,

61
00:03:49.959 --> 00:03:53.274
named locations and the
tenant restrictions policy;

62
00:03:53.274 --> 00:03:57.570
Network Access Read All and Read
Write All for signaling and tagging;

63
00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:01.488
and Policy Read Write Conditional
Access to create the policy,

64
00:04:01.488 --> 00:04:05.850
with the two administrator roles
activated just for the window.

65
00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.912
Lab 1, after listing the reads
and collecting the pilot tenant.

66
00:04:13.912 --> 00:04:16.874
Step 3 checks the
compliant network policy.

67
00:04:16.874 --> 00:04:22.900
It's built right: the location, the block,
both exclusions, no Private Access app.

68
00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:25.987
And it has been in report-only
for ten business days.

69
00:04:25.987 --> 00:04:30.126
But it isn't ready: the service
carried three report-only failures.

70
00:04:30.126 --> 00:04:33.575
Step 4 reads the guide's
query to see why that matters.

71
00:04:33.575 --> 00:04:36.654
Forty-one failures came
from users off the service,

72
00:04:36.654 --> 00:04:39.000
which is exactly what the policy is for.

73
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.710
The three with through
equal true are defects:

74
00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:44.910
users on the service who
would have been blocked.

75
00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:51.147
Step 5 checks the tenant restrictions.

76
00:04:51.147 --> 00:04:53.673
The default blocks, and tagging is on.

77
00:04:53.673 --> 00:04:58.271
But one partner tenant is allowed with
no register entry, and the block query

78
00:04:58.271 --> 00:05:02.797
shows a tenant that isn't a registered
partner, listed for the weekly review.

79
00:05:02.797 --> 00:05:04.062
Step 6 plans ring 1,

80
00:05:04.062 --> 00:05:08.698
whose desired state wants the
compliant network policy enforced.

81
00:05:08.698 --> 00:05:10.484
Without the enforce switch,

82
00:05:10.484 --> 00:05:14.135
the plan leaves it report-only
and says so on a manual line.

83
00:05:14.135 --> 00:05:19.770
A reviewer passes the switch only once step
3's check is clean, which it isn't yet.

84
00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:28.332
Step 7 checks the same policy two
weeks later, after ring 1's collection.

85
00:05:28.332 --> 00:05:31.295
It's enforced, and the
check that only applies

86
00:05:31.295 --> 00:05:34.257
to an enforced policy
passes: signaling is on.

87
00:05:34.257 --> 00:05:36.344
The unregistered partner is gone,

88
00:05:36.344 --> 00:05:40.369
and the blocked tenant is still
listed for review, which is its job.

89
00:05:40.369 --> 00:05:44.945
Step 8 runs this chapter's
tests: five passed, none failed.

90
00:05:44.945 --> 00:05:47.150
Then delete the output folder.

91
00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:55.515
Lab 2 needs a test tenant with the
Microsoft profile on for a pilot group,

92
00:05:55.515 --> 00:06:00.600
a test device, an emergency access group,
and a second test tenant as the partner.

93
00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:04.587
Find the two Intune apps and
put their IDs in the settings.

94
00:06:04.587 --> 00:06:09.026
Collect, plan and apply the report-only
policy with the approved hash.

95
00:06:09.026 --> 00:06:14.101
Run Learn's test: sign in, disable the
client, open a new browser session,

96
00:06:14.101 --> 00:06:18.287
and sign in again; in report-only,
that's a recorded failure.

97
00:06:18.287 --> 00:06:22.888
Read the tenant restrictions policy,
and stop if its default doesn't block.

98
00:06:22.888 --> 00:06:24.676
Run Learn's four-case test.

99
00:06:24.676 --> 00:06:27.212
Then back it out: delete the lab's policy,

100
00:06:27.212 --> 00:06:29.987
and turn tagging off if
the lab turned it on.

101
00:06:29.987 --> 00:06:32.360
The authors haven't run this lab.

102
00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:41.485
The reviewed policy file supports access
enforcement and session authenticity.

103
00:06:41.485 --> 00:06:44.339
Reading report-only
results before enforcing

104
00:06:44.339 --> 00:06:47.485
supports session termination
and impact analysis.

105
00:06:47.485 --> 00:06:51.238
A blocking default, registered
partners and tagging support

106
00:06:51.238 --> 00:06:54.623
the controls on external
systems and information flow.

107
00:06:54.623 --> 00:06:56.184
The assessor decides them.

108
00:06:56.184 --> 00:07:00.995
The dangers: enforcing before the client
is everywhere, forgetting the Intune

109
00:07:00.995 --> 00:07:03.364
exclusions so new devices can't enroll,

110
00:07:03.364 --> 00:07:06.472
and tagging with a default
that allows everything.

111
00:07:06.472 --> 00:07:10.916
A Global Secure Access outage is a
danger too: Microsoft's break-glass

112
00:07:10.916 --> 00:07:15.048
scripts are rehearsed, and Chapter
8's ring record holds the date.

113
00:07:15.048 --> 00:07:17.620
You're done after a
clean report-only period,

114
00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:21.122
an enforce decision on the
record, and an owned partner list.

115
00:07:21.122 --> 00:07:23.000
Next, Chapter 6.
